--TEST--
Core\Db::quoteIdentifier refuses every name that would need a delimiter to be legal, so nothing in its answer can end the identifier and start something else — `rule:security/sink-predicate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// What laundering owes `rule:security/sink-predicate` is that the result cannot end the
// identifier and start something else. The member buys that with an alphabet
// rather than with escaping: no quote, no backtick, no bracket, no space, no
// dot, no semicolon and no comment opener survives, so there is nothing left in
// the answer for any of the five dialects' parsers to act on.
//
// Counted rather than read off a line, so a name the member quietly accepted
// fails here even though every other name in the sweep behaved.

array<string> $refused = [
    "users; DROP TABLE t",
    "users\"",
    "\"users\"",
    "`users`",
    "[users]",
    "users t",
    "public.users",
    "users--",
    "users/*",
    "users)",
    "users'",
    'user$id',
    "naïve",
    "",
];

int $caught = 0;
foreach ($refused as string $name) {
    try {
        Core\Db::quoteIdentifier($name);
    } catch (LogicError $bad) {
        $caught = $caught + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused=", $caught, "/14\n";

// `$` is on that list although four of the five backends accept one inside an
// identifier: it is the character that also opens PostgreSQL's dollar-quoting,
// and one rule a caller can hold in their head is worth more than a character
// almost nothing names a column with.
try {
    Core\Db::quoteIdentifier('user$id');
} catch (LogicError $dollar) {
    echo $dollar->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused=14/14
Core\Db::quoteIdentifier() takes a bare identifier — a letter or `_`, then letters, digits or `_` — and `user$id` is not one. `Core\Db` has no connection and so no dialect to delimit for, so a name that needs delimiting is refused rather than quoted for the wrong backend
